+
+## Land a kit material's detail frequency on THIS artifact's size.
+##
+## PbrKit's triplanar is LOCAL and its uv1_scale is tiles per metre, so a builder
+## tuned for a 2 m panel puts its grain somewhere else entirely on a 0.6 m box.
+## scale_detail MULTIPLIES, so the factor is derived from whatever tiling the
+## builder chose (uv1_scale.x, the across-grain component in every builder used
+## here) rather than assigned over the top of it: each material keeps its own
+## character and only the frequency moves, and it moves to one shared number.
+##
+## This is also why brushed_metal, terminal_body and anodized appear NOWHERE in
+## this file. Their stretch is (3, 40, 3) — a brush direction, not a tiling — and
+## scale_detail scales uniformly, so fitting the 3 up to 6 would drive the 40 to
+## 80 and put the streaks well under a pixel. That is television static, not
+## steel. Every metal here is machined_metal, which is isotropic.
+func _fit_grain(m: StandardMaterial3D, tiles: float = GRAIN_TILES_PER_M) -> StandardMaterial3D:
+	if m == null:
+		return m
+	var base: float = maxf(m.uv1_scale.x, 0.001)
