+## A projected contact pool under a curated artifact, on the plinth cap or the
+## platform deck it was just grounded to.
+##
+## Grounding is the one thing this composer does that nothing else can check: it
+## measures each artifact's AABB and drops it onto the cap by its own base_y. The
+## maths has always been right and it has never LOOKED right, because a thing
+## with no shadow under it hovers however correct its Y is. A Decal conforms to
+## whatever it lands on — a plinth cap, a tray, a platform deck — instead of
+## z-fighting the way a flat quad would, and it is not a light, so it costs
+## nothing against the VR light budget. One per grounded item, at most five.
+func _ground_pool(x: float, z: float, y: float, cells: int) -> void:
+	# Under a cap of `cells` metres, so the pool always ends inside the plinth's
+	# tray lip rather than running off the edge of what the thing stands on.
+	var r: float = clampf(float(maxi(cells, 1)) * 0.42, 0.18, 2.6)
+	# Parented under a pivot at the contact plane, the way fire_extinguisher and
+	# exit_sign do it: ground_shadow gives the Decal its own lift so the
+	# projection box hangs ABOVE the surface, and moving the node itself would
+	# sink half that box under the cap.
+	var pivot := Node3D.new()
+	pivot.name = "Contact"
+	pivot.position = Vector3(x, y, z)
+	var d: Decal = PBR.ground_shadow(r, 0.5, 0.04)
+	# Keeps the pool off vertical faces — the tray lip on a plinth cap, and the
+	# curated artifact's own sides, which are inside the projection box.
+	d.normal_fade = 0.55
+	pivot.add_child(d)
+	add_child(pivot)
